      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the number of different settings for the suitcase lock when all three digits must be different, we need to consider the following steps:\n\n1. **Choosing the first digit:**\n   The first digit can be any of the digits from 0 to 9. Therefore, there are 10 possible choices for the first digit.\n\n2. **Choosing the second digit:**\n   The second digit must be different from the first digit. Since there are 10 digits in total and one of them has already been used as the first digit, there are 9 remaining choices for the second digit.\n\n3. **Choosing the third digit:**\n   The third digit must be different from both the first and second digits. Again, since there are 10 digits in total and two of them have already been used as the first and second digits, there are 8 remaining choices for the third digit.\n\nTo find the total number of possible settings, we multiply the number of choices for each digit together:\n\n\\[\n10 \\text{ (choices for the first digit)} \\times 9 \\text{ (choices for the second digit)} \\times 8 \\text{ (choices for the third digit)} = 720\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of different settings for the suitcase lock is \\(\\boxed{720}\\).",
