    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "This is a problem from combinatorics and takes into play permutations. Each of the three digits for the locker has out of 10 options. The first digit has 10 options, the next 9, and last 8. So 10 x 9 x 8 = 720\n\nNow your turn to approach the problem. Think how to label everything fairly.\n\nTo solve the problem of finding how many different settings are possible for a suitcase lock with three different digits, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Label the Dials**: Assume that each dial is labeled from \\(0\\) to \\(9\\).\n2. **Select the First Digit**: For the first digit, there are \\(10\\) possible choices (since any digit from \\(0\\) to \\(9\\) can be used).\n3. **Select the Second Digit**: After choosing the first digit, there are \\(9\\) remaining choices for the second digit because all digits except the first must be different.\n4. **Select the Third Digit**: After choosing the first and second digits, there are \\(8\\) remaining choices for the third digit to ensure all three digits are different.\n\nNow, you multiply the number of choices for each step:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total number of settings} = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8\n\\]\n\nPerform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n\\]\n\\[\n90 \\times 8 = 720\n\\]\n\nSo, the number of different settings possible for the suitcase lock is \\(\\boxed{720}\\).",
