    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of different settings for a suitcase lock with 3 dials and each dial can be set to any digit from 0 to 9, with the requirement that all three digits must be different, we can proceed as follows:\n\n1. **First Dial Choice**: \n   There are 10 possible choices for the first digit.\n\n2. **Second Dial Choice**:\n   After placing a digit on the first dial, 9 digits remain available (since it must be different from the first digit).\n\n3. **Third Dial Choice**:\n   After placing two digits on the first and second dials, 8 digits remain available (since it must be different from both previously set digits).\n\nThus, we calculate the total number of different settings by multiplying these choices:\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Total Settings} = (\\text{Choices on First Dial}) \\times (\\text{Choices on Second Dial}) \\times (\\text{Choices on Third Dial}) \n\\]\n\nSubstituting values into these expressions:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times 9 \\times 8 = 720\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of different settings is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{720}\n\\]",
